<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard Rumbold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1622">1622</a>–<a href="/years/1685">1685</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard Rumbold was an English Parliamentarian soldier and political radical. He served in the New Model Army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a>, was one of the guards at the execution of Charles I in January 1649 and reached the rank of captain before he was dismissed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration">Restoration of 1660</a>. Implicated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">Rye</a> House Plot of 1683, an alleged plan to assassinate Charles II and his brother James, he escaped to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>'s Rising of 1685. Captured after being badly wounded, he was executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> on 26 June 1685; words of his scaffold speech were later quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Rumbold's background beyond the year of his birth and his family's roots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, possibly at Royston. He joined the Parliamentarian army in 1642 and the New Model Army when it was formed in 1645. At some point he lost an eye, perhaps in battle, and his friends called him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>. He was a Baptist, a sect strong in the New Model, and was closely associated with the Leveller movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In February 1649 Rumbold was among those who petitioned for the Agitators to be restored to the Army Council, but he seems to have drawn back when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> turned against them, for his name is missing from the version the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levellers">Levellers</a> printed, and he was rewarded with a commission. He fought at the battles of Dunbar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> and was a lieutenant in Cromwell's Regiment of Horse by 1651. After his dismissal in 1660 he married a widow who owned a malting business at Rye House, near Hoddesdon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>.</p><p>He remained active among radicals, many of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a> and army veterans, who campaigned from 1679 to 1681 to exclude the Catholic James from the succession and helped organise the Great Petition of 1680 for the recall of Parliament, signed by 18,000 people. The Rye House Plot took its name from his home: Charles and James were to be ambushed there on their way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from Newmarket in March 1683, and Rumbold was reportedly to kill the king, though how serious the plan was is still debated. In exile he joined Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>, and sailed with him in 1685.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a> was captured on 18 June 1685 the rebels were told to disperse. Intercepted by militia near Lesmahagow on the night of 20 to 21 June, Rumbold killed one man and wounded two before his horse was shot under him. He was tried and executed on the same day, allegedly so that his wounds should not kill him first. On the scaffold he denied any plan to murder the king and declared that none comes into the world with a saddle on his back, words that echoed John Harris and were quoted at the Convention that drew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a> in 1787 and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> in a letter of 24 June 1826.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Richard Rumbold” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Royston, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.05,-0.02&amp;year=1662" aria-label="Royston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Richard Rumbold"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4999 5678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4999 5678h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.05,-0.02&amp;year=1662">What was here in 1662? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
